# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HRIV Trivy ignore list
#
# Trivy consumes this file (via the `trivyignore:` input of the shared
# bcit-tlu/.github `oci-build.yaml` reusable workflow, called per image from
# .github/workflows/ci.yaml) and suppresses the listed CVEs from both the
# PR-time table scan and the main/tag SARIF upload. Entries here are
# *explicit, documented, reviewed decisions* — each group below has a
# justification comment and is dated for review.
#
# Review cadence: quarterly.
#   Last reviewed:   2026-09-25
#   Next review due: 2026-12-25
#
# Review procedure (per image, from a fresh build of the `stable` target):
#   trivy image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH --ignorefile /dev/null <img>
#   → drop every group whose packages/CVEs no longer appear, and check
#     https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-... for the rest.
#   trivy image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH --ignore-unfixed <img>
#   → must be 0. Every entry in this file is an *unfixed* upstream CVE; a
#     CVE with a fixed version must be fixed by upgrading, never ignored.
#
# Adding an entry requires:
#   1) A justification comment referencing *why* the CVE is non-reachable, not
#      fixable, or an accepted residual risk in HRIV's specific deployment.
#   2) A group header so the entry is discoverable when reading the file top
#      to bottom — don't just append a bare CVE-ID at the bottom.
#   3) The CVE's severity as a trailing `# sev=...` comment so at-a-glance
#      review surfaces the ones that are critical/high.
#
# Removing an entry is *free* — the next Trivy scan will re-report the CVE.
# Prefer removing an entry whenever the justification no longer holds (e.g.
# if HRIV starts shipping a codec delegate that was previously disabled in
# backend/Dockerfile, the matching ignore block must go).
#
# Removal-before-suppression: every package below was checked for removal
# from the runtime image before being accepted here (apt-get -s purge,
# apt-cache rdepends --installed, ldd). None can go: the util-linux /
# ncurses / perl-base / libacl1 / libsystemd0 packages are Essential or
# Pre-Depends of bash and util-linux; the backend X11 / libxml2 / libtiff /
# libexpat libraries are DT_NEEDED by the custom libvips.so.42 (via
# libopenslide / libcairo / libfontconfig); the backup image's perl is what
# postgresql-client-common's pg_wrapper (/usr/bin/pg_dump, /usr/bin/psql)
# is written in.
#
# NOTE: the backend image deliberately does NOT install `libvips42t64` from
# Debian — it builds `libvips` from source with unused codec delegates
# compiled out (see `libvips-build` stage in backend/Dockerfile).  The RAW /
# HEIF / JPEG-XL / OpenEXR / ImageMagick / MATLAB / Poppler / GnuPG CVE
# groups that used to dominate this file are *physically absent* from the
# runtime image and no longer need ignore entries.
#
# Runtime entrypoints referenced by the justifications below (none of the
# images run an interactive shell, cron daemon, or login service):
#   backend             uvicorn app.main:app / arq worker (python:3.13-slim, Debian 13 trixie)
#   backup              opentelemetry-instrument python backup.py (python:3.13-slim-trixie)
#   restore-validation  python restore-validation orchestrator (python:3.13-slim-trixie)
#   synthetic-monitoring npx playwright test (mcr.microsoft.com/playwright:*-noble, Ubuntu 24.04)
#
# Format reference: https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy/latest/docs/configuration/filtering/#trivyignore
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------


# === Debian trixie base packages (backend, backup, restore-validation) ========
# The three Python images share python:3.13-slim (Debian 13 "trixie"). The
# groups in this section are the trixie base layer; the same CVE is reported
# once per binary package built from the same source package, so one CVE-ID
# here silences up to nine alerts per image (e.g. the util-linux family).


# --- util-linux family: local-privilege / interactive-tool CVEs ---------------
# CVE-2026-76642, CVE-2026-78408, CVE-2026-78409, CVE-2026-78410 are in the
# util-linux 2.41 source package and are reported against every binary built
# from it. They concern the setuid/interactive tools (mount/umount, su, login,
# lslogins, chfn/chsh) and require a local shell in the container. HRIV
# images have no interactive entrypoint and no login path, and the backup
# image runs as a non-root UID with a read-only root filesystem
# (charts/backup/values.yaml securityContext); the shared libraries (libblkid1,
# libmount1, libsmartcols1, libuuid1) are only loaded by those same tools —
# neither CPython nor libvips links them. util-linux is Essential: yes and
# cannot be purged.
# Debian status: trixie 2.41.5-0+deb13u1 vulnerable, <no-dsa> (minor issue);
# fixed only in forky/sid 2.42.3-1 as of 2026-09-25.
# https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-76642
#
# Packages: util-linux, bsdutils, mount, login, libblkid1, libmount1,
#           libsmartcols1, libuuid1, liblastlog2-2
#           (scope: backend, backup, restore-validation)
CVE-2026-76642  # sev=high
CVE-2026-78408  # sev=high
CVE-2026-78409  # sev=high
CVE-2026-78410  # sev=high

# --- util-linux / shadow: chfn/chsh/login info leaks (low/medium) ------------
# CVE-2022-0563 (chfn/chsh readline info leak) and the sibling login/passwd
# entries require an interactive `chfn`/`chsh`/`login` session. No such
# session can exist in HRIV containers (see entrypoints above). Debian has
# declared these unreachable in container contexts and does not intend to fix.
#
# Packages: util-linux, bsdutils, mount, login, passwd, libblkid1, libmount1,
#           libsmartcols1, libuuid1, liblastlog2-2
#           (scope: backend, backup, restore-validation; `login` also in
#            synthetic-monitoring's Ubuntu base)
CVE-2007-5686   # sev=low
CVE-2022-0563   # sev=low
CVE-2024-56433  # sev=low
CVE-2026-3184   # sev=medium

# --- ncurses: terminfo parser out-of-bounds read --------------------------------
# CVE-2025-69720 is in the terminfo database/`tic` parsing path and is
# triggered by loading an attacker-supplied terminfo entry (TERMINFO /
# ~/.terminfo). No process in HRIV images runs with a controlling terminal:
# uvicorn/arq, backup.py and the restore-validation orchestrator never load
# terminfo, and `pg_dump`/`psql` are run non-interactively via subprocess.
# libtinfo6 is a Pre-Depends of bash and util-linux and ncurses-base /
# ncurses-bin are Essential, so the family cannot be purged; CPython's
# `_curses`/`readline` extension modules are the only other consumers and
# HRIV never imports them. No fix in trixie-security as of 2026-09-25.
#
# Packages: ncurses-base, ncurses-bin, libncursesw6, libtinfo6
#           (scope: backend, backup, restore-validation)
CVE-2025-69720  # sev=high

# --- perl: perl-base (all three) + full perl (backup only) ---------------------
# CVE-2026-9538 is in the perl interpreter/core modules. perl-base is
# Essential in Debian and cannot be removed. The backup image additionally
# carries perl/perl-modules-5.40/libperl5.40 because postgresql-client-common
# implements /usr/bin/pg_dump and /usr/bin/psql as the `pg_wrapper` perl
# script that backup.py invokes; `apt-get -s purge perl` would take
# postgresql-client-16 with it. The only perl that ever runs is pg_wrapper,
# with argv fully controlled by backup.py (no user-supplied input, no
# network-facing perl). No fix in trixie-security as of 2026-09-25.
#
# Packages: perl-base (backend, backup, restore-validation);
#           perl, perl-modules-5.40, libperl5.40 (scope: backup)
CVE-2026-9538   # sev=high

# --- libacl1: POSIX ACL parsing --------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-54369 is reachable only through `setfacl`/`getfacl` or an
# application calling acl_from_text() on attacker-controlled ACL text. HRIV
# never manipulates ACLs; libacl1 is present only as a dependency of
# coreutils/tar (Essential). No fix in trixie-security as of 2026-09-25.
#
# Packages: libacl1  (scope: backend, backup, restore-validation)
CVE-2026-54369  # sev=high

# --- systemd libraries: libsystemd0 / libudev1 ---------------------------------
# CVE-2026-16742 concerns systemd/udev device and journal handling. The
# images have no systemd, udevd or journald process — libsystemd0/libudev1
# are shared libraries pulled in by util-linux/procps and are never invoked
# (no process calls sd_* or udev_* APIs). No fix in trixie-security as of
# 2026-09-25.
#
# Packages: libsystemd0, libudev1  (scope: backend, backup, restore-validation)
CVE-2026-16742  # sev=high


# === backend-only: libvips delegate dependencies =================================
# The backend builds libvips from source (`libvips-build` stage) with only the
# codecs in `backend/app/image_validation.py::IMAGE_EXTENSIONS` enabled. The
# libraries below are DT_NEEDED by libvips.so.42 (directly or via
# libopenslide0 / libcairo2 / libfontconfig1) and cannot be removed without
# dropping the OpenSlide delegate. Untrusted input reaches libvips only after
# `image_validation.py` has checked the extension/magic bytes, and image
# processing runs in the arq worker, not the API process.


# --- backend/libxml2: XML parser CVEs (incl. the one CRITICAL) -----------------
# libxml2 is linked into the backend image only through libopenslide0 (which
# uses it to read the XML metadata blocks of whole-slide formats) and
# libarchive13t64 / shared-mime-info. HRIV's accepted upload set is
# jpeg/png/tiff/webp/gif + OpenSlide slide formats; the only XML that is ever
# parsed is the embedded slide metadata of files that already passed
# `image_validation.py`, inside the arq worker. No user-supplied XML/HTML
# document reaches libxml2 from the API (FastAPI does not use libxml2; the
# Python stack has no lxml). CVE-2026-6653 (critical) is a parser
# memory-safety issue; the only caller is OpenSlide reading metadata of an
# already-validated slide file inside the worker.
# Debian status: trixie 2.12.7+dfsg+really2.9.14-2.1+deb13u3 vulnerable,
# <no-dsa> (minor issue); fixed only in forky/sid as of 2026-09-25.
# https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-6653
#
# Packages: libxml2  (scope: backend)
CVE-2026-6653   # sev=critical
CVE-2026-74860  # sev=high
CVE-2026-86138  # sev=high
CVE-2026-86139  # sev=high
CVE-2026-86140  # sev=high
CVE-2026-86142  # sev=high
CVE-2026-86143  # sev=high
CVE-2026-86144  # sev=high

# --- backend/libexpat1: XML parser CVEs -----------------------------------------
# libexpat1 is pulled in by libfontconfig1 (a libcairo2 → libopenslide0
# dependency) and by the glib build; HRIV never renders text (pangocairo and
# fontconfig delegates are disabled in libvips) so fontconfig never parses
# its XML config, and CPython's `pyexpat` is only used by the stdlib
# plistlib/xmlrpc modules HRIV does not import. No untrusted XML reaches
# expat. No fix in trixie-security as of 2026-09-25.
#
# Packages: libexpat1  (scope: backend)
CVE-2026-66046  # sev=high
CVE-2026-76956  # sev=high
CVE-2026-76957  # sev=high
CVE-2026-93990  # sev=high

# --- backend/libtiff6: TIFF codec CVEs -------------------------------------------
# libtiff is a required delegate (`.tif`/`.tiff` are in IMAGE_EXTENSIONS and
# OpenSlide's tiled-TIFF readers depend on it), so it cannot be removed.
# libtiff-tools (tiffcp/tiffcrop/tiff2pdf) is NOT installed; only the
# decoding library is present. Uploads are size-capped and validated by
# extension + MIME before decode, and decoding runs in the arq worker, not
# the API process. Accepted residual risk. No fix in trixie-security as of
# 2026-09-25.
#
# Packages: libtiff6  (scope: backend)
CVE-2026-36849  # sev=high
CVE-2026-52490  # sev=high

# --- backend/libx11 + libxrender: X11 client libraries ---------------------------
# libx11-6/libx11-data/libxrender1 are transitive dependencies of libcairo2
# (via libopenslide0). There is no X server and no DISPLAY in the container;
# libvips is built with pangocairo disabled and never opens an X connection,
# so the X11 wire-protocol parsing these CVEs live in is unreachable. Purging
# them would remove libopenslide0. No fix in trixie-security as of
# 2026-09-25.
#
# Packages: libx11-6, libx11-data, libxrender1  (scope: backend)
CVE-2026-88806  # sev=high
CVE-2026-88807  # sev=high


# === backend-only: Python packages ===============================================

# --- backend/ecdsa: Minerva timing side-channel (CVE-2024-23342) ----------------
# `ecdsa` is a hard dependency of `python-jose` (backend/pyproject.toml →
# python-jose[cryptography] → ecdsa). HRIV signs and verifies its own JWTs
# with HMAC only: `AuthSettings.jwt_algorithm` defaults to "HS256"
# (backend/app/auth.py) and every jwt.decode() call pins
# `algorithms=[auth_settings.jwt_algorithm]`; no ES256/ECDSA key material
# exists anywhere in the deployment, so the pure-Python ECDSA code the timing
# attack targets is never executed. Upstream has stated the issue will not
# be fixed in pure Python. Follow-up (tracked separately, not in this
# change): migrate python-jose → PyJWT/joserfc, which drops the ecdsa
# dependency entirely.
#
# Packages: ecdsa (python)  (scope: backend)
CVE-2024-23342  # sev=high


# === synthetic-monitoring (Ubuntu 24.04 noble, Playwright base image) ============

# --- gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad: media plugin CVE ---------------------------------
# CVE-2025-3887 is in the GStreamer "bad" plugin set shipped by the Microsoft
# Playwright base image as a WebKit media dependency. HRIV's synthetic
# journeys (`npx playwright test`) run headless Chromium only
# (synthetic-monitoring/playwright.config.ts `projects`) against HRIV's own
# pages; no media element is played, no WebKit browser is launched, and the
# image processes no external media files. No fixed version for noble as of
# 2026-09-25.
#
# Packages: gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad, libgstreamer-plugins-bad1.0-0
#           (scope: synthetic-monitoring)
CVE-2025-3887   # sev=high
